WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.766 --> 00:00:02.649
- [Narrator] The year was 1996.

00:00:02.649 --> 00:00:04.387
It was a different era, before memes,

00:00:04.387 --> 00:00:06.566
before viral videos,

00:00:06.566 --> 00:00:09.176
before anybody argued over
whether it's a giff or a jiff.

00:00:09.176 --> 00:00:10.744
It's GIF, Jiff is peanut butter.

00:00:10.744 --> 00:00:12.618
There was the dancing baby.

00:00:12.618 --> 00:00:16.285
(upbeat instrumental music)

00:00:19.407 --> 00:00:20.690
What is the dancing baby?

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:23.109
Who better to ask than its
father, Michael Girard?

00:00:23.109 --> 00:00:25.529
- What are you doing here?

00:00:25.529 --> 00:00:27.678
- [Narrator] The dancing baby, remember?

00:00:27.678 --> 00:00:28.774
- Oh, the dancing baby.

00:00:28.774 --> 00:00:31.821
Robert Lurye And I worked together

00:00:31.821 --> 00:00:33.649
to design the animation.

00:00:33.649 --> 00:00:37.200
The idea was to show that
movement could be programmed

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.567
and projected onto different characters.

00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:42.003
- [Narrator] So Autodesk, the company

00:00:42.003 --> 00:00:43.011
that Michael worked for,

00:00:43.011 --> 00:00:44.133
started sending around the software

00:00:44.133 --> 00:00:45.930
to other developers and animators.

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:47.369
It was just another demo
film until it landed

00:00:47.369 --> 00:00:49.886
on the Lucas Arts server.

00:00:49.886 --> 00:00:51.051
It was there that Ron Lussier tweaked

00:00:51.051 --> 00:00:53.600
the original file,
converted it into a GIF,

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.570
and sent an email that would
change his life forever.

00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:59.054
So we thought we should ask
Ron about his experience,

00:00:59.054 --> 00:01:02.721
but Ron Lussier would
only talk for $30,000.

00:01:04.376 --> 00:01:05.971
Every time the “you've got mail” voice rang.

00:01:05.971 --> 00:01:09.146
- [Man] Welcome, you've got mail,

00:01:09.146 --> 00:01:11.694
file's done, goodbye.

00:01:11.694 --> 00:01:14.253
- [Narrator] It was another
goddamn dancing baby.

00:01:14.253 --> 00:01:18.240
- Even I got an email of
the goddamn dancing baby.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.488
- [Narrator] From that point
on, it was unstoppable.

00:01:20.488 --> 00:01:22.072
Everywhere you looked
there was a dancing baby,

00:01:22.072 --> 00:01:24.815
on television, on t-shirts,
on ties and commercials,

00:01:24.815 --> 00:01:25.815
even boxers.

00:01:27.289 --> 00:01:28.122
- Boxers?

00:01:29.028 --> 00:01:32.491
- [Narrator] Yes Michael, boxers.

00:01:32.491 --> 00:01:33.674
This all begs the question, what is it

00:01:33.674 --> 00:01:36.148
about the dancing baby that
makes it so irresistible?

00:01:36.148 --> 00:01:38.553
And what does it say about us?

00:01:38.553 --> 00:01:40.693
- Have you heard of the uncanny valley?

00:01:40.693 --> 00:01:45.243
It's a paradox where the more
a computer generated figure

00:01:45.243 --> 00:01:49.999
resembles a human, the more
we become revolted by it.

00:01:49.999 --> 00:01:52.474
- [Narrator] The dancing
baby is the uncanny valley.

00:01:52.474 --> 00:01:55.891
- The dancing baby is the uncanny valley.

00:01:56.809 --> 00:01:57.814
- [Narrator] It's been over two decades

00:01:57.814 --> 00:01:59.131
since Girard and his team unleashed

00:01:59.131 --> 00:02:00.504
their monstrosity on the world.

00:02:00.504 --> 00:02:02.244
Does he regret it?

00:02:02.244 --> 00:02:03.661
- Yes, 100%.

00:02:05.327 --> 00:02:09.077
(upbeat instrumental music)

